We use Redis for temporary and fast storage like OTPs and rate limiting.
When a user requests an OTP, we first check Redis to see whether the user already requested one within the last 60 seconds. If yes, we block the request to prevent spam.

If allowed, we generate a 6-digit OTP and store it in Redis with an expiry time of 5 minutes using TTL.

Instead of sending the email directly from the API, we use RabbitMQ for asynchronous processing. The API publishes an email task into a queue and immediately returns a response to the user, making the API very fast.

RabbitMQ consumers run separately in the background and continuously listen to the queue. When a new message arrives, the consumer processes it and sends the OTP email.

We use assertQueue to ensure that the queue exists before publishing or consuming messages
