          “In Node.js, JavaScript code first goes to the V8, which converts the code into machine code that the computer understands. Node.js runs JavaScript on a single main thread, which means it executes one task at a time on the call stack. If the task is synchronous or blocking, like readFileSync, Node.js waits until the task finishes before moving to the next line. But if the task is asynchronous or non-blocking, like database calls, file uploads, APIs, or timers, Node.js sends that work to background systems such as OS APIs or libuv worker threads. While the background task is running, the main thread stays free and continues handling other requests. Once the background task finishes, its callback is added to the callback queue. The event loop continuously checks whether the call stack is empty, and when it becomes free, it takes the callback from the queue and executes it. This is why Node.js can handle many requests efficiently even though JavaScript execution itself is single-threaded.


